WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.420
Sup man.

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:02.520
How you doing today sir?

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.300
Good.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.520
You uh, you a citizen?

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.950
That's my business.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:07.880
You've probably seen them before.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.460
Go ahead and go over there, behind that other
vehicle if you'll do me a favor.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.620
That's, no thanks.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:17.440
Internal border checkpoint refusals, now a
popular YouTube genre.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:23.020
I don't want to hold up traffic I want to
freely travel down the road, may I do that?

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:23.680
You may.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:24.600
Some are funny.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.200
What's up, are you an American citizen?

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:26.860
A US Citizen?

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:27.960
Are you American citizen?

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:28.460
Yes sir.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.500
Do you have an ID on you?

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:31.500
Some are hostile.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.039
And some, like the story we are about to tell
you, result in weeks of jail time

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.540
without a single charge being prosecuted.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:44.260
Get out!

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.559
I was born in 1984 and back then it was like
Soviet Union

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.140
I moved to US I thought, you know, I'm going
to get all my rights, my freedoms, my liberty,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.639
you know, American dream.

00:01:00.639 --> 00:01:03.979
Greg Rosenberg is a naturalized US citizen

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:08.600
who immigrated to America 10 years ago from
Armenia.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.280
Memories and family stories of Soviet rule
in his country of origin

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.780
infused him with strong political beliefs
and a reverence for individual rights.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:26.080
Today I see a lot of similarities about the
stories that my parents were telling me about the past

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.400
if you complained about the government, back
then they were taken to Siberia

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.020
you don't have a right here too to legally
challenge the government.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.340
So perhaps it's no surprise that Greg is a
checkpoint protester.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.900
As a long haul trucker, he has to drive through
these all the time.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.260
After all, there are about 170 of them scattered
across the United States.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.120
Completely doesn't make any sense to have
these interior checkpoints

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.579
if they are saying they want to protect, you
know, the border

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.900
it should be on the border and not inside
America like violating our rights,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:01.940
freedom of movement.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:06.160
Keep in mind, these checkpoints aren't on
an actual border,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.180
they just have to be within 100 miles of one.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.480
Counting the northern border, southern border
and the coastlines

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.340
the ACLU estimates that roughly two thirds
of the nation's population

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.260
lives within this one hundred mile zone.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.240
Greg's ordeal began in the border town of
Laredo Texas.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.460
He and his friend pulled onto I 35 North around
midnight on September 26th

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.060
in a truck carrying a load of xerox machines
destined for Fort Worth.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:40.340
But only 29 miles north of the border they'd
encounter the Laredo North Border Patrol station.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:45.960
And Greg would undergo a checkpoint interrogation
like he'd never experienced before.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.680
The immigration officer asked us if we are
US citizens.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:55.660
The driver, my friend, he was cooperative,
he was going to answer it.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.680
But I started to talk.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.360
Greg says that border patrol took his cell
phone

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.220
and tried to delete all the footage of the
encounter that he's describing.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:09.319
But he was able to use software to recover
the last two minutes before his arrest.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.840
I said to the immigration officer

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.200
'excuse me but we have a legal right not to answer your question.'

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.120
So he went and he asked his supervisor

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:33.079
so the supervisor came in, he asked me to
pull over the truck to the secondary lane

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:37.120
and because there was no probable cause I
refused to do so.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:53.000
And the supervisor said 'hey if you don't
move the truck to the secondary lane

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.320
then I'm going to arrest you.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.140
They opened the door, they drug me out, it
was like six of them.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.320
Right after that video when they drag me out
I was saying 'hey I'm not resisting it'

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.980
cause they were handcuffing me and they were
trying to hurt me.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:45.160
I didn't get it cause couldn't have imagined
challenging government's actions

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:51.020
talking about constitution, you know, about
your rights that could get you arrested.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.780
This was only the beginning of Greg's nightmare.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.560
Border Patrol detained and continued to question
Greg and his friend

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.500
about their citizenship at the station.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.640
They were able to verify that he was a citizen
using his ID

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:06.700
but Greg says they wanted something more.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:10.760
Basically was saying that if I agree that
I'm wrong, that I will never do it again

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.960
that I would apologize, you know, give up
my rights, my values,

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.860
so I would never come back again to the checkpoints
but I couldn't do it.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:22.500
Also he's like 'look at you, what do we think
where you from originally?'

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.300
he's like 'yeah you see, after 9/11 that's
why we are doing it.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.220
We asked Border Patrol, Immigration and Customs
Enforcement

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:36.960
and the Southern District of Texas' US Attorney's
Office to give their account of events for this video

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.140
They all refused.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.400
Most of them, they are, they were just laughing,
having fun of it

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:47.639
you know, they were applauding, clapping their
hands and like making fun of the situation

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:50.620
cause I was saying 'hey you can't just arrest
innocent people

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:55.120
you know just because they talk about the
constitution or they want to exercise their rights.'

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.720
And it was funny for them.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:02.940
According to Greg it was after he declined
to apologize for inconveniencing the border agents

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:07.460
that the station chief booked him for impeding
border patrol duties.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:13.380
This offence fell under a broad resisting,
assaulting or impeding arrest statute,

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:14.340
a felony.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:20.360
When I first got arrested, I asked to I have
a lawyer I'm not going to give a statement.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.300
I saw my lawyer after two weeks.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:28.260
On October 10th, a full 19 days after Border
Patrol detained Greg along I 35

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:32.800
the government dropped its case and dismissed
all charges.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.580
The problem here is that the government drops
charges because there is no case against Greg

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.880
because there is no evidence that he resisted
arrest, because he didn't do that.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:46.400
Prama Lal is an immigration attorney representing Greg
in a counter suit against the government.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:51.900
The fact that he spent 19 days in jail is
a violation of his rights

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.260
it's false imprisonment, that's actually a
civil rights violation.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:00.900
There's a particularly important moment in
Greg's video legally speaking.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:01.720
Remember this?

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.760
The agent says this Supreme Court case, United
States v Martinez Fuerte

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.700
means it's reasonable for them to be there
asking questions.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:23.900
Greg says they're allowed to ask the question
but he doesn't have to answer.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:25.660
The Supreme Court ruled that

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:29.509
'The Border Patrol's routine stopping of a
vehicle at a permanent checkpoint

00:07:29.509 --> 00:07:35.740
located on a major highway away from the Mexican
border for brief questioning of the vehicle's occupants

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:38.120
is consistent with the Fourth Amendment.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:44.629
But there's a catch, checkpoint searches are
constitutional only if justified by consent

00:07:44.629 --> 00:07:46.720
or probable cause to search.

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:51.000
Any further detention must be based on consent
or probable cause.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.140
They never stated any probable cause or documented
it.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:08:00.800
And by simply dismissing the charges and walking
away after imprisoning Greg for 19 days

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:04.400
the government essentially punted on the central
legal question.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:10.860
Obviously there's a conflict here whereby,
yes CBP can ask people about their citizenship status

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:17.240
but people don't have to answer and that leads
to a back and forth that you may have seen.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:20.220
YouTube videos across the internet really.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.250
Most people, most times people are let go.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:25.220
-looking around my vehicle, I'm free to go?

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:26.040
Have a good day.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.940
I mean I have my theories about why there
are differently,

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:33.080
I mean, at least when I watched the video,
Greg's a US citizen

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:36.360
but he's a naturalized US citizen and he has
an accent.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.620
If you don't move, I'm going to arrest you
right now.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:39.360
For what, did I, did I-

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:40.800
For impeding my duties!

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:41.440
What duties?

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:43.480
Is this a border?

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:47.160
And I believe it played out this way because
of racial profiling.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:49.210
They might be arresting me for that in the
beginning

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:53.930
but they probably would let me go, if I just
bow down and, you know,

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:58.070
because I say I'm a libertarian, I'm a principled
person, I don't like government actions

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.080
but I like you guys I have no problem.

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:04.520
They hated me for that, so they arrested me
what I look like

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.240
but they pressed the charges because of my
beliefs.

